ge, such as the union bound or the target test, is assumed."},{"obj_id":"lem:transfer-hall","verdict":"matched","note":"Encodes Hall's matching step in the Hall-criterion context of prop:transfer. The objects are: J, Y and a witness G from TransferConfiguration (all of ass:transfer-configuration); U, the positive-degree J-vertices outside Y; and candidate sets cand w ⊆ [n] \\ Y. The Hall inequality |U'| ≤ |⋃ cand| for every U' ⊆ U is taken as a hypothesis, since proving it is prop:transfer's job, as the passage says. The conclusion is a map f injective on U with f w ∈ cand w for every w, i.e. the matching to distinct vertices of [n] \\ Y. The paper's candidate sets are its usable sets, and the Lean allows any candidate sets inside the outside vertices. That only makes the lemma more general, so it still gives the paper's claim. The extra configuration and subset hypotheses are the paper's standing context. Nothing beyond the matching is assumed."}],"assumption_verdicts":[],"substrate_gates":[{"name":"Papers.OaiGraphDecompositionsIntegral.transfer_hoeffding","statement":"∃ c > 0, for all n, r, s satisfying TransferParameters, every Y with |Y| ≤ n^(2/3) and 2|Y| ≤ n, and every admissible Q with xQ r Q ≥ n^(-1/100): Pr over G(n,r) of ¬SourceTest ≤ exp(-c n^(98/100)).","classical_fact":"Hoeffding (1963) Theorem 2, eq. (2.6), sum form. The summands f_Q(N_G(w) ∩ Y) for w ∉ Y are independent, lie in [0,1] and have mean x(Q). Taking deviation t = x(Q) over m ≥ n/2 summands gives exp(-2m x(Q)^2) ≤ exp(-n^(98/100)).","missing_infra":"","gate_class":"cited","source":{"cite_id":"cite:hoeffding63","locator":"Hoeffding's inequality"},"check_status":"cited-verified-attested"},{"name":"Papers.OaiGraphDecompositionsIntegral.transfer_hall","statement":"For U a subset of the positive-degree J-vertices outside Y and candidate sets cand w ⊆ [n] \\ Y: if every U' ⊆ U has |U'| ≤ |⋃_{w ∈ U'} cand w|, then there is an f injective on U with f w ∈ cand w.","classical_fact":"Hall (1935) Theore